(CHAPTER IV.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(TAXATION OF THE PEASANT.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When the balance of a peasant farm is closed, year in, year out, with a) Tj T*
(deficit, it is only of secondary importance whether there be added to) Tj T*
(it a score of rubles or not, in taxes. In either case the farmer has) Tj T*
(to look for employment outside of his homestead that he may be able) Tj T*
(to keep body and soul together. Nor is it of great moment that the) Tj T*
(taxes must be paid in money, since at any rate not a small part of the) Tj T*
(produce must be carried to the market to be converted into money for) Tj T*
(the purchase of implements, clothing, and even of food for the peasant) Tj T*
(and his cattle.[43] But the economic influence of taxation is marked) Tj T*
(by its compulsory character, as well as by its unequal pressure upon) Tj T*
(different classes of the people.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It may be regarded as an established rule that the burden of taxation) Tj T*
(is, in Russia, in inverse ratio to the means of the taxpayer.[44]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The former serf is taxed more, absolutely \(every male and every) Tj T*
(worker\), and relatively \(every acre of land\), than is the former State) Tj T*
(peasant. The difference is literally the tribute paid to the landlord) Tj T*
(class for the emancipation of their serfs.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Indeed, the greater part of the contributions of the former serf is) Tj T*
(composed either of his redemption tax, or of the payment due to his) Tj T*
(master \(_taille_\):) Tj T*
