(chained the Russian peasant to the soil.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER V.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(COMMUNAL TENURE AND SMALL HOLDINGS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Two economic features determined the further development of) Tj T*
(Russia, after the abolition of serfdom. Personal dependence of the) Tj T*
(serf was replaced, as above shown, by economic dependence of the) Tj T*
(?peasant-proprietor? compelled to seek work for wages beyond the limits) Tj T*
(of his own holding. Inequality of condition among the peasants, created) Tj T*
(by legal discrimination and furthered by the fiscal system, furnished) Tj T*
(the basis for the division of labor by which the peasants tried to fill) Tj T*
(up the holes in their farming. What were these occupations, and how did) Tj T*
(they react upon the village community?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the times of serfdom the village community, as above mentioned,) Tj T*
(enjoyed certain rights to the land which was used by the master) Tj T*
(himself. Pasture, and water, and way in the landlord?s estate were) Tj T*
(free to the community. The emancipation deprived the peasants of) Tj T*
(these privileges and put them under the necessity of entering into) Tj T*
(agreements, of one kind or another, with the landlord for the use of) Tj T*
(these easements.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Where lack of water, or the necessity of a way through the landlord?s) Tj T*
(estate, has been artificially created by the reform, it is obviously) Tj T*
(the community as a whole that must contract the agreement.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In so far, however, as rented pasture is concerned, the usual) Tj T*
(communistic rule is put on trial by the growing inequalities that have) Tj T*
(arisen in the business of stock breeding within the village community.) Tj T*
(About one fourth of the community is composed of the poorest families,) Tj T*
(who own no horses, and oftentimes no cattle at all.[53] It is obvious) Tj T*
(that whenever the use of a pasture is rented for horses or cows, a not) Tj T*
(inconsiderable part of the community is practically excluded from the) Tj T*
(agreement. The assessment of the obligation in proportion to the shares) Tj T*
(held by the several householders in the communal land would be unjust) Tj T*
(to the poorest part of the community.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another basis for the distribution is found, in many instances, in) Tj T*
(the number of heads of cattle belonging to each householder, _i. e._) Tj T*
(outside of the province of agrarian communism; the poor are thus) Tj T*
(released from the burden of payments. But, on the other hand, the) Tj T*
(community becomes virtually the voluntary partnership of its wealthier) Tj T*
(members. The economic tendency of the time is shown by the following) Tj T*
(figures:[54]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
