(We notice that the greater the percentage of separations during the) Tj T*
(period from 1877 to 1887, the smaller the average plot per family and) Tj T*
(per worker, and _vice versâ_. About one-half of the households whose) Tj T*
(plots are the smallest, are those who have separated in the course of) Tj T*
(the last ten years and have as a rule only one worker. On the other) Tj T*
(hand, the largest plots, absolutely and relatively, are held by the) Tj T*
(compound families of the old stamp, of whom only about one-quarter) Tj T*
(have undergone division during the last decade.[100]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Furthermore we find a certain percentage of the village community) Tj T*
(absolutely without any land: Thus we have--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                       Per cent.) Tj T*
(  In Ranenburg           4) Tj T*
(  In Dankoff             4) Tj T*
(  In Korotoyak           1.7) Tj T*
(  In Nizhnedevitsk       0.5) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This new class of the peasantry owes its existence solely to the) Tj T*
(division of the family:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
