(CHAPTER XI.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(INDIVIDUAL OWNERSHIP AND AGRARIAN COMMUNISM.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thus far we have seen the changes which the parcelling of soil wrought) Tj T*
(in the constitution of the village population. We are now brought) Tj T*
(face to face with the question of how small peasant landholding is) Tj T*
(influenced by this parcelling.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In countries with individual property in land, the question is settled.) Tj T*
(In Russia the case is complicated by the system of communal ownership) Tj T*
(in land.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Yet the right of alienation, the main essential for the question at) Tj T*
(issue, is inherent in quarterly possession on an equal footing with) Tj T*
(private property. Thus we can avail ourselves of the opportunity for) Tj T*
(comparative study.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Quite naturally, the distribution of land shows more irregularity under) Tj T*
(quarterly possession than under agrarian communism.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
