(area. In a region confined mainly to agriculture, landholding is the) Tj T*
(determining factor of economic life. Should we find the same condition) Tj T*
(of landholding amidst similar surroundings, physical, geographical and) Tj T*
(legal, we might be justly entitled to assume throughout identity of) Tj T*
(economic structure. Such is virtually the case as regards the ?central) Tj T*
(black soil, prairieless zone,? which has been the main seat of famine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It may, therefore, reasonably be assumed that economic conditions in) Tj T*
(middle Russia about 1881 were essentially the same as in the region) Tj T*
(here described, allowance being made for numerical fluctuations.) Tj T*
(It was at this date that revolutionary peasantism had reached its) Tj T*
(climax, and to cope with it, a new era of ?national policy? was) Tj T*
(inaugurated by Count Ignatieff. The question now arises as to whether) Tj T*
(counter-influences had arisen which exercised a neutralizing effect) Tj T*
(upon the economic tendencies that developed during the reign of) Tj T*
(Alexander II. A full discussion of the economic policy of the present) Tj T*
(Russian government would carry us beyond the limits of the present) Tj T*
(treatise.[177] I shall confine myself, therefore, to a few remarks) Tj T*
(relative to the two state institutions created for the encouragement) Tj T*
(of agriculture, viz: The Nobility?s Crédit Foncier, and the Peasant?s) Tj T*
(Crédit Foncier.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Hundreds of millions were appropriated in the course of a few years) Tj T*
(to prevent the complete ruin of the landholding nobility. No such) Tj T*
(liberality was allowed in the conduct of the Peasant?s Bank, which) Tj T*
(was founded with the express object of providing the money needed by) Tj T*
(the peasant for the purchase of land.[178] Amidst the jubilations) Tj T*
(with which the peasantist press greeted the birth of this still-born) Tj T*
(child, Mr. Lobachevsky \(pseudonym\), one of the broadest minded of) Tj T*
(the Russian statisticians, raised the sole dissenting voice. He) Tj T*
(advanced the opinion[179] that to establish a Bank with a stock of a) Tj T*
(few millions for tens of millions of peasants, was to create a small) Tj T*
(peasant _bourgeoisie_ that would inevitably take advantage of the) Tj T*
(poverty of the more helpless members of its class, and that the poor) Tj T*
(householder would infallibly succumb if he accepted the services of) Tj T*
(the Peasant?s Bank. This opinion received a speedy confirmation in the) Tj T*
(actual practice of the Bank, which soon proved itself to be merely a) Tj T*
(supplementary department of the Nobility?s Bank.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Says Mr. Herzenstein, a Russian Catheder-Sozialist, ?It is universally) Tj T*
(known that the peasants? purchases enabled the landlords to get rid, at) Tj T*
(a high price, of those tracts which yielded them no income, and that,) Tj T*
(taking it all in all, the peasants paid more for their land than it was) Tj T*
(worth.?[180]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was again the same truly Russian system which had been tried with) Tj T*
(such splendid success on the occasion of the emancipation of the serfs.) Tj T*
(Furthermore, the interest levied by the Bank, viz: 7½ per cent.,) Tj T*
(exceeds that charged by any of the private mortgage banks \(6 per) Tj T*
(cent.\), whereas, with the Nobility?s Bank, the interest is less than) Tj T*
(that charged by private banks.[181]) Tj T*
