(It is therefore by no means surprising to find that speedy ruin is the) Tj T*
(debtor?s fate. In the period from 1887 to 1890, 8.8 per cent. of all) Tj T*
(the land purchased with the aid of the Peasant?s Bank, was relinquished) Tj T*
(by the mortgageors, the failures amounting to 7,637,034 rubles, or to) Tj T*
(14 per cent. of all the loans granted by the Bank.[182] The operations) Tj T*
(of the Bank necessarily suffered a diminution.[183] However, all these) Tj T*
(inconveniences are but matters of secondary importance. Had everything) Tj T*
(gone smoothly, the Bank would nevertheless have effected no actual) Tj T*
(change in the economics of the village.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As may be remembered, the village community needs about one-half) Tj T*
(more land in order to enable all its members to hold their position) Tj T*
(as farmers. To put peasant landholding upon a proper footing in the) Tj T*
(famine-stricken region, many times more land would be required than) Tj T*
(that purchased by all the peasants throughout Russia with the aid of) Tj T*
(the Peasant?s Bank.[184]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It may be questioned whether the operations of the Bank have been) Tj T*
(even sufficient to counterbalance the further parcellation of peasant) Tj T*
(holdings which has resulted from the growth of population. The economic) Tj T*
(tendencies prevalent in the village during the first year of the) Tj T*
(present reign may be regarded as being even more pronounced to-day.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The present catastrophe was consequently by no means unexpected, and) Tj T*
(there has been no lack of alarming symptoms within the past ten years.) Tj T*
(In 1883, 1884 and 1885 famine stalked alternately through western) Tj T*
(Siberia, through the northeast, and through certain of the central) Tj T*
(provinces of European Russia \(Vyatka, Kazañ, Kursk, etc.\). Famine) Tj T*
(was again reported in 1889.[185] To such an extent was the peasantry) Tj T*
(already exhausted that even the extraordinarily good harvest of) Tj T*
(1890[186] was unable to prevent a subsequent failure of crops from) Tj T*
(resulting in a famine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is only in the area affected that the present failure is) Tj T*
(distinguished from its precursors.[187] The cause of the various) Tj T*
(famines is at bottom always essentially the same, viz: the backwardness) Tj T*
(of Russian agriculture. The surface of the soil has become finally) Tj T*
(exhausted and the wooden plough of the Russian peasant is unable to) Tj T*
(reach down to the deeper layers where the soil is yet virgin. Deep) Tj T*
(ploughing is impossible with only one horse, and that horse fed on) Tj T*
(straw. It is further not only the peasant land, but also the major) Tj T*
(part of the landlord?s fields, that is cultivated with the peasant?s) Tj T*
(stock and implements. Thus the crisis of peasant agriculture is at the) Tj T*
(same time the crisis of Russian landlord farming.[188] The famine) Tj T*
(has brought about at one single stroke the dissolution which had been) Tj T*
(slowly going on in the village since 1861.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Russian papers have published a multitude of letters from their) Tj T*
(correspondents telling of the loss of some 50% of the horses owned) Tj T*
(by the peasants. This means the complete ruin of the weak groups) Tj T*
(of the village, and the further concentration of the communal land) Tj T*
(into the hands of the strong, who alone survived as the farming) Tj T*
