(of very recent date. In Siberia it cannot be traced farther back than) Tj T*
(two generations, and there are even now a great many districts in which) Tj T*
(no limitations are imposed by the community on the free use of land) Tj T*
(by every one of its members. Nevertheless the poll tax was applied to) Tj T*
(these districts also for about two centuries. It seems to prove that) Tj T*
(the imposition of the said tax did not necessitate subdivision except) Tj T*
(where land was scarce. It may consequently be inferred that it was not) Tj T*
(the poll tax, but the scarcity of land in the most crowded provinces,) Tj T*
(that prompted the subdivision. In this view the subdivision of the land) Tj T*
(appears to be a natural phase in the evolution of communal landholding.) Tj T*
(\(With reference to this point _cf._ Prof. W. J. Ashley?s remarks in his) Tj T*
(introduction to Fustel de Coulanges? _The Origin of Property in Land_,) Tj T*
(pp. xlvii-xlviii.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([22] Mr. Pankeyeff makes in one passage an allusion to the analogy) Tj T*
(between the development of quarterly landholding into agrarian) Tj T*
(communism and the transformation of the right of first possession into) Tj T*
(communal ownership in New Russia and in the _gubernia_ of Voronezh) Tj T*
(\(_Cf._ _op. cit._, book III., p. 35\). The analogy, however is not) Tj T*
(further worked out.) Tj T*
