([169] The socialistic aversion of the Russian peasantists to the) Tj T*
(?exploiters? was somewhat tainted with the patrician prejudices) Tj T*
(against the merchant. The Russian magazines were crammed with touching) Tj T*
(descriptions of how the poetry of a shadowy oak alley in the old) Tj T*
(garden of the noble slave-owner was ruthlessly sacrificed in favor) Tj T*
(of prosaic timber by the boorish parvenu \(_tchoomáziy_\). It was) Tj T*
(universally believed that the merchant who engaged in land tenure was) Tj T*
(something of a dynamiter, whose element was destruction for the mere) Tj T*
(devilish voluptuousness of destruction. To devastate the forests while) Tj T*
(re-renting the land to the peasant at an exorbitant interest--this) Tj T*
(appeared to be the only aim of the merchant. Statistical investigations) Tj T*
(did away with these naive conceptions. Here are some of the facts) Tj T*
(brought to light by the Ryazañ census:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Bailiwick Naryshkinskaya, d. Ranenburg._ ?The lack of land to rent) Tj T*
(is keenly felt. The condition of the communities under discussion has) Tj T*
(grown much worse as compared with former years. The main reason thereof) Tj T*
(is the considerable decrease in the area leased by landlords and the) Tj T*
(rise of rental prices, which is closely connected with the passage of) Tj T*
(the estates of the nobility into the hands of merchants through either) Tj T*
(sale or lease.? \(_L. c._, vol. II., part I., p. 282. No. 3-4, 6-9.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _Village Prosech?ye, same district._ ?Since their former master sold) Tj T*
(his estate to the merchant, neither land nor easements are to be got) Tj T*
(anywhere. The new owner cultivates everything for himself.? \(_L. c._,) Tj T*
(p. 305, No. 13.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _Village Cheglokovo, b. Vednovskaya._ ?The condition of the peasants) Tj T*
(grew much worse after their former master sold his estate, about) Tj T*
(1870, to a merchant, who has almost entirely stopped leasing land.) Tj T*
(The master, on the contrary, used to lease much of his land, and the) Tj T*
(peasants assert that they then made a pretty good living.? \(_Ib._, p.) Tj T*
(325, No. 5. _Cf._, also, Nos. 6, 7.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. _B. Troitskaya._ ?Tenure is a rare exception, since the landlords) Tj T*
(either carry on their own farming or have leased their estates to big) Tj T*
(farmers, who cultivate everything for themselves.? \(_Ib._, p. 309.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. _B. Hrushchovskaya, Dankoff._ ?All the landlords in the neighborhood) Tj T*
(either carry on their own farming, or have leased their estates to) Tj T*
(merchants, who cultivate solely for themselves. The peasants can) Tj T*
(positively get no land for rent, except a small tract of meadow.? \(_L.) Tj T*
(c._, part II., p. 208. _Cf._, also bailiwick _Ostrokamenskaya_, p. 211,) Tj T*
(and b. _Odoevskaya_, p. 230.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
