([191] Fertilizing and irrigation have become a necessity in Russian) Tj T*
(agriculture. Let us figure the expenses entailed by these improvements.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We know that manure is procured for the landlord?s fields by the) Tj T*
(decaying small farmer. The ruin of the latter necessitates an outlay) Tj T*
(of capital by the landlord for the purchase of live stock. Now, to) Tj T*
(fertilize the fields once in three years, 2 heads of big cattle are) Tj T*
(required per dessiatine of arable land, which would cause an expense) Tj T*
(of 78.96 rubles per dessiatine. \(_Cf._, _Statistical Reports for the) Tj T*
(Gubernia of Voronezh_, Vol. II., Number II., App., pp. 44-45.\) Here) Tj T*
(we have the Achilles heel of the Russian landed nobility. The land) Tj T*
(acquired by the peasants with the aid of the Peasants? Bank sold at an) Tj T*
(average price of rubles 43.41 the dessiatine. \(Herzenstein, _l. c._,) Tj T*
(p. 104\). The cost of fertilizing alone exceeds the total value of the) Tj T*
(land; it could consequently not be conducted on a large scale by means) Tj T*
(of funded loans.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The conditions are similar in the case of irrigation. Mr. Vladimir) Tj T*
(Biriukowicz, a writer in the _Russkaya Mysl_, quotes a few instances) Tj T*
(of how artificial irrigation has increased the rental value of the) Tj T*
(estates from 3 rubles to 15, and even 25 rubles yearly per dessiatine.) Tj T*
(Moreover, and this is of greater importance, amidst the surrounding) Tj T*
(failure, the irrigated estates were blessed by excellent crops.) Tj T*
(According to Mr. Daniloff, a civil engineer, irrigation had raised the) Tj T*
(productivity of ploughland by from 15 to 20 per cent., and of meadow by) Tj T*
(100 per cent., while the cost of construction did not exceed 60 rubles) Tj T*
(per dessiatine. \(_l. c._, April 1, 1892, _Protection and Agriculture_,) Tj T*
(pp. 2, 3.\) Certainly there is nothing exorbitant in the expense;) Tj T*
(still it likewise requires an outlay of capital exceeding the value) Tj T*
(of the land, and this, in the opinion of a practical agriculturalist,) Tj T*
(must be accounted for as the chief reason of the indifference of the) Tj T*
(landlords in the matter of irrigation. \(_Cf._, ?Topographical Surveying) Tj T*
(for irrigation works,? by V. Kasyanenko. _The Agriculturist_, St.) Tj T*
(Petersburg, No. 47, 1892\). Thus the progress of artificial irrigation) Tj T*
(means the ruin of the nobleman.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([192] I am glad to know that this is the opinion advanced by so high) Tj T*
(an authority in political economy as Mr. Frederick Engels, one of) Tj T*
(the few Western students familiar with the Russian language. \(_Die) Tj T*
(Neue Zeit_, 1892.\) So far, however, as my case is concerned, I claim) Tj T*
(independence of judgment. I wrote in an editorial, dated December 20,) Tj T*
(1891: ?The consequences of this famine are equivalent to a revolution) Tj T*
(in the social organization of the Russian village.? The development) Tj T*
(of capitalism in agriculture, the dissolution of the peasantry into) Tj T*
(two distinct groups: a rural _petite bourgeoisie_, and a rural) Tj T*
(proletariat--these are the characteristics of a new epoch in Russia?s) Tj T*
(social life.? \(_Cf._, _Progress_, No. 3, a Russian weekly published at) Tj T*
(the time in New York.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
